rder. Self-loops and parallel edges are ignored, and an isolated vertex is a clique of one","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"(NetworkX ","type":"text"},{"code":"find_cliques","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", igraph ","type":"text"},{"code":"maximal_cliques","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", JGraphT’s Bron–Kerbosch finders).","type":"text"}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/maximalCliques()","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"references":{"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/MaximalCliques":{"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MaximalCliques","kind":"identifier"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/MaximalCliques","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/maximalcliques","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MaximalCliques","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"MaximalCliques","kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Every maximal clique of an undirected graph’s simple graph once, lazily, each in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"order (by vertex index): an isolated vertex, or one whose only edges are loops, is a clique of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"one. The order is Eppstein–Löffler–Strash’s: by the cliques’ first vertex in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"coreNumbers().degeneracyOrdering"},{"type":"text","text":", then as Bron–Kerbosch with Tomita’s pivot (ties to the least"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"index) and branches in ascending index meets them. The sequence holds the graph, so iterating"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"again gives the same cliques. O(n + m) before the first clique, O(d·n·3^(d\/3)) in all for"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"degeneracy d."}],"type":"topic"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques":{"role":"collection","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/docume